Z||000012||AusFiero||05-30-2001||06:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RRobinson:<BR><B>#'s sold new in Canada:<P>1086 87 GTs<BR>423 88 GTs<BR>139 Formulas<BR>168 88 Coupes<P>Source: GM Historical services<P>RR</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>And 1 86 GT Fastback.<BR>So where does my mid to late 86 Fiero GT Fastback fit into this equation? Is a 5 speed GT Was definately sold new in Canada as I bought it off the original Canadian owner. Still have the factory KMH speedo etc. The t-tops were fitted straight after purchase and I believe at the time had just been released by cars and concepts at that time. A Canadian fitter ftted the t-tops in the cars first week of life with its last owner.<P>Now I am really interested how it has been sold new there before they was released?<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000013||RRobinson||05-30-2001||09:25 AM||||Aus,<BR>I don't know the '86 GT number yet. I got my information from GM Canada through a vehicle report they sell for $75CDN. You give them your VIN and you get a neat little package with the RPO codes, production date, delivery date, original dealer etc, and production numbers for the SAME MODEL NUMBER ONLY. ie GT/SE/Sport coupe/coupe. I've been meaning to send away for all my cars, but it gets expensive.<BR>RR<BR>2-88 GTs<BR>2-87 GTs<BR>87 SE V6<BR>86 GT 5-speed ||||reg||1
